# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the GPU tests and the FFmpeg tests (used by the thread-sanitizer
# preset only). Most entries are called_from_lib: entries (one deadlock: entry, see below). A
# called_from_lib: entry makes ThreadSanitizer skip every intercepted
# call made from code inside the named library (a substring of the loaded file's path, e.g.
# /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libvulkan_lvp.so): its memory accesses (malloc, free, memcpy, memcmp,
# ...) and its synchronization (mutexes, condition variables, semaphores). Thread creation and join
# stay visible. Unlike race:, it does not hide races in dvv code on memory the library allocated or
# on threads it created, so dvv code that the library calls back stays checked.
# - A race between dvv code and an access made by the library stays hidden.
# - The library's locks are invisible: a callback that the library's threads run must synchronize
#   with dvv threads through dvv primitives (atomics or dvv mutexes), or it is reported as a race.
# - The library's own mutexes and condition variables are invisible too: a hand-over between dvv
#   threads through a Vulkan wait (fence, queue, timeline) must also go through a dvv primitive, or
#   it is reported as a race.
# - The ignore can create lock-order reports through libraries the ignored one calls (libLLVM,
#   libstdc++, later libX11/libxcb): such a library seems to keep or lose a lock. Suppress each with
#   a narrow named deadlock: entry on that library's frame, never on a dvv frame.
# - ThreadSanitizer aborts the process when a matched library is unloaded or one entry matches two
#   loaded files (see the unload notes below).
#
# Vulkan drivers (here Mesa lavapipe) are not instrumented: they synchronize their own objects with
# primitives ThreadSanitizer cannot see (futex-based locks), so concurrent, valid calls are reported
# as races inside the driver. Seen without this entry (rawreel.gpu; the cli_e2e GPU tests report
# nothing): a data race, memcmp against calloc, both called from libvulkan_lvp.so on two dvv
# ThreadPool threads that record conversions at once (dvv::media ToDisplay::run, resumed by
# Submitter::complete); the number of reports depends on timing (1 in the last check). With this
# entry no race is reported, so no race: entry is needed.
# The ignore hides an unlock. Inferred, NOT VERIFIED (perhaps a tail call to pthread_mutex_unlock
# that ThreadSanitizer attributes to lavapipe): with this entry ThreadSanitizer sees
# llvm::MCJIT::RegisterJITEventListener (libLLVM, called by lavapipe when dvv creates a device)
# take a mutex but never sees it released, so the main thread seems to hold it until exit, and a later
# Vulkan loader lock (seen: vkDestroyDevice) closes a lock-order cycle through it. Without the
# deadlock: entry: 7 lock-order-inversion reports (3 in rawreel.gpu, 1 each in cli_e2e.gpu, .bench,
# .oversize and .ffmpeg_gpu), each with that frame. A deadlock: entry matches a substring of the
# function, file or module of any frame in any recorded lock-acquisition stack
# (second_deadlock_stack=0) or thread creation stack; it can mask a real dvv cycle only if that
# cycle goes through a lock taken inside RegisterJITEventListener. A Mesa that renames the frame
# (ORC JIT) makes CI fail visibly.
# Unload: the ICD manifest names libvulkan_lvp.so, so the loader opens it through the library
# search path (/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libvulkan_lvp.so on Ubuntu 24.04, where /lib links to
# /usr/lib); the entry matches either form. The tests create and destroy Vulkan instances, and the
# runs report no "is unloaded" abort: no abort was seen (lavapipe has no NODELETE flag and the
# loader may dlclose ICDs; why it stays mapped is not known). A newer Mesa or loader that really
# unmaps it aborts these tests; then go back to race:libvulkan_lvp.so.
called_from_lib:libvulkan_lvp.so
deadlock:llvm::MCJIT::RegisterJITEventListener
# FFmpeg's shared libraries are not instrumented: libavcodec's slice and frame threads synchronize
# their own buffers with primitives ThreadSanitizer cannot see (atomics and progress flags in the
# uninstrumented code), so the only accesses it sees from them are the intercepted calls (memset,
# memcpy, malloc and free inside libavcodec and libavutil), and valid decoding is reported as races
# between FFmpeg's threads and with the dvv code that reads a decoded frame after FFmpeg has handed
# it over. Unload: the tests that use this file must not reach the loader's paths that close FFmpeg
# libraries again (a partial, refused or lavfi-incomplete FFmpeg build); see
# libs/dvv-components/tests/tsan.supp for what stays checked and what stays hidden. Same list.
called_from_lib:libavutil.so
called_from_lib:libavcodec.so
called_from_lib:libavformat.so
called_from_lib:libswscale.so
called_from_lib:libswresample.so
called_from_lib:libavfilter.so
